  <h1>Proof and Correction Ledger</h1>
  <p class="lede muted">A correction does not merely alter a formula. It changes which later conclusions
    can still use that formula. This ledger follows five headline claims through the archive and stops each
    argument at the first step not supplied.</p>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame frame--first illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">Audit rule</p>
    <h2 id="t-rh-audit-rule">A proof must survive its own revisions</h2>
    <p class="lede">The archive deserves credit for recording errors. Consolidation must now propagate each
      correction forward instead of leaving the original theorem label in place.</p>
    <p>A numerical result can support a conjecture, test an implementation or expose a false generalisation.
      It cannot replace a missing uniform bound. A conditional Hadamard formula can describe what follows if
      every zero is on the critical line. It cannot be used as the unconditional starting point of an argument
      meant to prove a property without RH. A leading asymptotic can suggest a sign. It cannot determine a
      discrete sum unless the remainder is bounded more tightly than the proposed margin.</p>
    <p>This page uses “unproved” in that precise, local sense: the supplied documents do not close the claim.
      It does not assert that the claim is false, and it does not survey every result in the literature. Where
      the archive cites 2026 papers, their contents are treated as source reports until independently checked.
      <span class="hist">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p>The audit also distinguishes a failed <em>bridge</em> from a failed <em>identity</em>. The corrected
      inverse-Laplace formula may be exact while the proposed positivity proof fails. The Cauchy&ndash;Schwarz
      proof on the real axis may be valid while its hoped-for continuation to the critical line is unavailable.
      Keeping the surviving half is the purpose of a ledger.</p>
  </div>
</section>

