<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">Ledger entry 1</p>
    <h2 id="t-rh-cm-audit">Complete monotonicity: the corrected formula breaks the easy proof</h2>
    <p>The dedicated complete-monotonicity document first states</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      F(x)=(\log\xi)''\!\left(\frac12+\sqrt{x}\right)
      =2\sum_k\frac1{x+\gamma_k^2}.
    \]</p>
    <p>Every term on the right is completely monotone, so that expression would make the conclusion nearly
      immediate under the all-on-line zero representation. Sessions 16 and 17 correctly identify that it is
      not \(F\). The corrected RH-conditional formula is</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      F(x)=2\sum_k\frac{\gamma_k^2-x}{(x+\gamma_k^2)^2}.
    \]</p>
    <p>Individual terms in the corrected sum are not completely monotone on the whole positive axis. The old
      term-by-term proof is therefore gone. The document’s alternative says that a family of sums
      \(S_n(r)\) is positive; that larger \(n\) should be easier; and that finite “epoch verification”, a
      smooth zero-density model and an off-line perturbation estimate complete every case.</p>
    <p>Those ingredients do not yet form a proof. “Easier” is not an induction inequality. Finite epochs do
      not cover an unbounded parameter. A smooth approximation must carry an explicit error below the claimed
      margin uniformly in \(n\) and \(r\). The off-line correction must be derived from the actual quartet
      product without assuming a corresponding on-line zero at the same ordinate. The documents supply no
      single bound that does all of this.</p>
    <p>There is another logical warning. Positivity of all derivatives at \(x=0\), even if established, would
      not by itself imply the alternating derivative signs for every \(x>0\). Bernstein’s theorem can conclude
      complete monotonicity from a positive representing measure; it cannot be used to assume the measure
      whose positivity is the disputed claim.</p>
    <p><strong>Verdict:</strong> the corrected formula is retained conditionally. Unconditional complete
      monotonicity is an open claim in this corpus. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">Ledger entry 2</p>
    <h2 id="t-rh-density-audit">Bernstein density: the small-\(t\) inequality points the wrong way</h2>
    <p>Termwise inverse Laplace transformation of the corrected RH-conditional \(F\) gives</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \rho(t)=2\sum_k(2\gamma_k^2t-1)e^{-\gamma_k^2t}.
    \]</p>
    <p>The dedicated proof handles \(t>1/(2\gamma_1^2)\) correctly: then every factor
      \(2\gamma_k^2t-1\) is positive. For \(0&lt;t\le1/(2\gamma_1^2)\), however, it says that only the first
      term can be negative and that all \(k\ge2\) terms are positive. The condition for a negative term is</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \gamma_k&lt;\frac1{\sqrt{2t}}.
    \]</p>
    <p>As \(t\) decreases, the right side grows. More early zeros, not fewer, enter the negative block. At
      \(t=10^{-4}\), even \(\gamma_2\approx21.02\) gives
      \(2\gamma_2^2t-1&lt;0\). The proof’s claimed one-negative-term decomposition is therefore false over most
      of the interval it is meant to settle.</p>
    <p>A smooth density estimate may still suggest a positive total, and the archive reports positive samples.
      Those are useful numerical evidence. They do not repair the sign partition. The stated small-\(t\)
      asymptotic also deserves correction: applying \(-(1+2t\,d/dt)\) to a leading
      \(t^{-1/2}\log(1/t)\) term cancels the logarithmic factor rather than multiplying it by three. A new
      proof would need a controlled heat-trace asymptotic with a rigorous remainder or a separate positivity
      theorem for the entire sum.</p>
    <p><strong>Verdict:</strong> the density formula survives under RH and suitable interchange conditions;
      positivity for every \(t>0\) is numerically supported but not proved here. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">Ledger entry 3</p>
    <h2 id="t-rh-third-derivative-audit">The third derivative: smooth balance is not a discrete sign theorem</h2>
    <p>The archive proposes</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      (\log\xi)'''(\sigma)&lt;0\qquad\text{for every }\sigma>\frac12
    \]</p>
    <p>without RH. Under RH, differentiating a paired-zero expression reduces the question to the sign of a
      discrete sum with kernel \((3\gamma^2-r^2)/(r^2+\gamma^2)^3\). For small \(r\), each term has the
      desired sign. At larger \(r\), terms of both signs appear. The diary calculates a smooth approximation,
      observes an exact cancellation of its leading integral and finds a positive logarithmic correction.
      It also checks many finite epochs numerically.</p>
    <p>This is a plausible proof programme, not the proof announced in the title. The intermediate interval is
      computer-sampled rather than enclosed by certified interval bounds in the supplied files. Replacing the
      zero-counting measure by its main term requires partial summation with endpoint terms and an explicit
      error estimate valid over the entire claimed range. Most importantly, the sum written with positive
      ordinates \(\gamma_k\) assumes an all-on-line pairing. Removing RH requires treating arbitrary symmetric
      quartets from the start.</p>
    <p>The proposed unconditional extension compares a hypothetical off-line quartet with an on-line baseline
      at the same height and assigns a very small global error. That comparison does not identify a canonical
      on-line zero being perturbed, and its bound is not shown uniformly for \(\sigma\) that grows through the
      same scale as the hypothetical ordinates. A fixed tiny tail estimate cannot simply be compared with an
      on-line margin that itself tends to zero without a uniform ratio proof.</p>
    <p><strong>Verdict:</strong> retain the small-\(r\) sign calculation, exact kernel integrals and numerical
      exploration. Treat global unconditional negativity as unproved. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="grid two">
    <div class="frame card">
      <p class="kicker">Ledger entry 4</p>
      <h2 id="t-rh-kernel-audit">Pólya-kernel log-concavity</h2>
      <p>The archive writes \(\Phi=\sum_nf_n\), proves each positive summand \(f_n\) log-concave on the
        relevant half-line, and then uses a weighted-variance decomposition for \((\log\Phi)''\). This is a
        sensible route. The displayed variance bound replaces the infinite support of logarithmic slopes by a
        two-point distance involving the first two terms, without proving that all remaining slopes lie inside
        those endpoints or bounding their weighted tail.</p>
      <p>A sum of log-concave functions is not automatically log-concave. The numerical margin reported in the
        archive may be real, but a proof needs an explicit tail inequality or an applicable closure theorem.
        The later statement “each term is log-concave, therefore the sum is” repeats precisely the missing
        implication.</p>
      <p>Even if strict log-concavity of this particular \(\Phi\) is proved, the archive’s later Gaussian
        counterexample correctly shows that kernel log-concavity alone does not force the first Laguerre
        inequality of its Fourier transform everywhere.</p>
      <p><strong>Verdict:</strong> numerical claim with an incomplete supplied proof; no RH bridge.
        <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <p class="kicker">Ledger entry 5</p>
      <h2 id="t-rh-critical-converse">Critical-line positivity</h2>
      <p>Under RH, the paired Hadamard expression gives positive contributions to
        \((\log\xi)''(\tfrac12+it)\) away from its poles. The archive first claimed the converse: one off-line
        zero produces a large negative contribution at its height, so positivity forces every zero back to the
        line.</p>
      <p>Session 7 correctly retracts that proof. The sign of one contribution is not the sign of the total;
        the background from all other zeros must be bounded. A later F28 document asserts equivalence again but
        its own numerical table says deviations below a threshold are not detected by the test. That evidence
        cannot establish the claimed converse.</p>
      <p>A counterfactual zero move also has to preserve functional-equation symmetry, conjugation and
        multiplicity. Adding an off-line quartet without subtracting the corresponding original factor changes
        the number of zeros and the background. The archive notices this in one addendum, another reason the
        short converse fails.</p>
      <p><strong>Verdict:</strong> RH implies the displayed positivity in the RH product representation. The
        reverse implication is not proved by these files. <span class="myth">&#9670;</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">Two shorter corrections</p>
    <h2 id="t-rh-wedge-pade">The “Bernstein wedge” and the Padé closure</h2>
    <p>The archive says the Laplace representation converges in \(\Re(s)>1/2\) because, with
      \(y=s-1/2\), it contains \(e^{-y^2t}\). But \(\Re(y^2)>0\) describes sectors in the complex
      \(y\)-plane, not the entire half-plane \(\Re y>0\). On the critical line \(y=i\tau\), the factor is
      \(e^{\tau^2t}\). Under the RH-conditional density series, the large-\(t\) tail begins with decay like
      \(e^{-\gamma_1^2t}\), so small \(|\tau|&lt;\gamma_1\) is not automatically divergent. Singularities and
      analytic continuation still matter, but the stated “every non-zero critical point diverges” conclusion
      is too strong.</p>
    <p>The Padé experiment similarly deserves a narrower label. One \([14/14]\) approximant reportedly placed
      poles in the wrong locations. That closes one numerical attempt. It does not prove that real-coefficient
      Padé approximants cannot represent imaginary poles: \(1/(1+z^2)\) has real coefficients and poles at
      \(z=\pm i\). Failure of an implementation is valuable evidence about that implementation, not a theorem
      about every rational continuation scheme.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame frame--creed">
    <p class="kicker">What publication would require</p>
    <h2 id="t-rh-publication-gate">From research diary to mathematical paper</h2>
    <p>Publish the real-axis log-convexity argument only after checking its prior literature and fixing a
      standard normalisation. Publish the exact integral identities as supporting lemmas, not as evidence of
      RH. Present the complete-monotonicity, Bernstein-density and third-derivative statements as conjectures
      unless uniform proofs are supplied. Attach code, precision, truncation rules and certified error budgets
      to numerical tables. Remove “all approaches fail” language; the archive establishes only that its tested
      approaches did not cross the gap.</p>
    <p>Most importantly, keep the correction history visible. The diary’s willingness to retract is one of its
      strongest features. A polished paper should inherit that honesty rather than its most confident headings.</p>
    <p>Continue to <a href="/riemann-splice-questions/">The Splice Questions After RH</a>, where the same audit
      discipline is applied to TTI’s proposed gravitational and electromagnetic hierarchy.</p>
  </div>
</section>